 ## Overview
-EWA Splatting is a high-quality volume rendering technique that eliminates aliasing artifacts by replacing the standard footprint function with a band-limited resampling filter [S1, unofficial][S2][S8, S10, unofficial]. It solves the problem of incorrect visibility determination and high-frequency sampling errors inherent in basic splatting, where reconstruction kernels are assumed non-overlapping [S1, S8, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings [Splatting Without the Blur](1157-splatting-without-the-blur.md) and [Splat Radius Adjustment Based on Normal Angle](1264-splat-radius-adjustment-based-on-normal-angle.md), which rely on isotropic or angle-adjusted approximations, EWA Splatting computes anisotropic, perspective-correct 2D Gaussian footprints by convolving a 3D elliptical Gaussian reconstruction kernel with a 2D low-pass filter [S2][S8, S15, unofficial].
+EWA Splatting is a high-quality volume rendering technique that eliminates aliasing artifacts in splat-based rendering by replacing the standard footprint function with a band-limited resampling filter [S1, unofficial][S2][S8, S10, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visual artifacts caused by incorrect visibility determination and sampling rate mismatches in perspective projections, which occur because standard splatting assumes non-overlapping kernels [S1, S8, S15, unofficial]. Unlike basic splatting or methods that use uniform scaling, EWA Splatting handles arbitrary elliptical kernels without excessive blurring by combining an elliptical Gaussian reconstruction kernel with a Gaussian low-pass filter [S2][S5, S8, S15, unofficial]. It builds on [Splatting](23-splatting.md) by introducing a mathematically derived 2D footprint that accounts for perspective distortion and Nyquist frequency limits.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Volume data represented as a set of particles (voxels), each with a position $\mathbf{p}_k$, extinction coefficient $g_k$, emission color $c_{\lambda k}$, and a 3D elliptical Gaussian reconstruction kernel defined by a variance matrix $\mathbf{V}_k$ in object space. Camera parameters: viewing transformation matrix $\mathbf{W}$ (rotational part) and projection matrix.
-**Outputs:** A 2D image with intensity values $I_\lambda(\hat{\mathbf{x}})$.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** Volume data represented as a set of particles (voxels), each defined by a position $\mathbf{p}_k$, a scalar density/extinction coefficient $g_k$, and a 3D elliptical Gaussian reconstruction kernel with variance matrix $\mathbf{V}_k$ [S6, S8, unofficial][S11].
+*   **Output:** A 2D image where each pixel color is the result of compositing the projected splats [S6, S8, unofficial][S11].
 
